## Main Events and Factors Leading to Collapse

The collapse was primarily triggered by a liquidity crisis centered around FTX’s native token, FTT. Binance’s announcement on November 6, 2022, to liquidate its FTT holdings caused the FTT price to plummet, sparking a withdrawal rush from customers[^7][^9]. FTX’s inability to cover an estimated $8 billion shortfall between owed funds and available assets became apparent during this period[^7][^11].

Leaked documents and reports revealed that Alameda Research was incorrectly using customer funds for risky loan activities instead of transferring them to FTX[^7]. FTX aggressively expanded through acquisitions and marketing, while employing risky trading strategies and relying heavily on its FTT token issued in 2019[^7]. The financial interdependency between Alameda and FTX deepened the crisis, as Alameda's balance sheet showed dangerous overconcentration in FTT, a token lacking substantial external market support[^13].

The lack of robust financial and risk management controls, along with inappropriate use of customer deposits for Alameda’s high-risk bets, formulated the underlying factors behind the failure[^14][^15].

