## Timeline of Critical Developments

- **Nov. 2, 2022:** Coindesk reported that Alameda Research held a large amount of FTT, indicating intertwined finances and a looming cash crunch[^8].
- **Nov. 6, 2022:** Binance declared plans to liquidate all FTT holdings, causing the token’s price to collapse[^9].
- **Nov. 7-9, 2022:** Massive customer withdrawals overwhelmed FTX. Binance initially signed a letter of intent to acquire FTX on Nov. 8 but withdrew the next day after reviewing FTX’s finances, citing issues beyond their control[^10][^11].
- **Nov. 10, 2022:** Crypto lender BlockFi paused client withdrawals in response to FTX’s crisis[^12].
- **Nov. 11, 2022:** FTX filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy, Sam Bankman-Fried resigned, and John J. Ray III was appointed CEO. The filing revealed over 130 affiliated entities with assets and liabilities estimated between $10 billion and $50 billion[^13].
- **Nov. 17, 2022:** John Ray III publicly condemned FTX’s operational failures under Bankman-Fried, highlighting poor security and misuse of funds[^14].
- **Nov. 30, 2022:** Bankman-Fried admitted mistakes but denied intentionally misusing client funds[^15].
- **Dec. 12, 2022:** Bankman-Fried was arrested in the Bahamas[^16].
- **Dec. 13, 2022:** U.S. prosecutors charged Bankman-Fried with multiple financial crimes relating to fraud, money diversion, and political donations[^17].
- **Dec. 22, 2022:** His parents posted $250 million bond for his house arrest in California pending trial[^18].
- **Aug. 11, 2023:** Bankman-Fried’s bail was revoked due to attempts to influence witnesses[^19].
- **Oct. 3, 2023:** Jury selection began for his trial[^20].
- **Oct. 27, 2023:** Bankman-Fried testified, acknowledging failures but denying fraud[^21].
- **Nov. 3, 2023:** He was convicted of fraud for stealing at least $10 billion from customers and investors[^22].
- **Mar. 28, 2024:** Sentenced to 25 years in prison; bitcoin prices recovered significantly[^23].
- **Apr. 30, 2024:** Binance founder Changpeng Zhao was sentenced to four months in prison for failing to prevent illicit use of the platform[^24].

