## Reasons for Financial and Operational Failure

FTX’s failure stemmed from systematic commingling of customer assets with Alameda Research's holdings along with a profound absence of financial controls and poor risk management[^14]. Approximately $8 billion of user deposits were illegally funneled from FTX to Alameda Research to cover risky bets[^14][^15]. The illicit transfer directly precipitated FTX’s liquidity crisis.

The intertwined finances of FTX and Alameda were further destabilized by Alameda's excessive concentration in FTT tokens, which lacked meaningful market demand or utility outside the FTX ecosystem[^13]. Unlike disciplined financial institutions, Alameda’s portfolio was a "disorganized collection" of speculative tokens and ventures without a coherent strategy, while FTX operated devoid of adequate risk assessments or protective internal controls[^15].

Locating FTX's headquarters in the Bahamas allowed it to circumvent multiple U.S. regulatory obligations, complicating oversight efforts[^16]. Concurrently, FTX engaged in extensive political influence efforts in the United States to obtain favorable regulatory positions, despite having poor operational risk management[^16].

