## Headquarters, Jurisdiction, and Legal Proceedings

FTX was headquartered in the Bahamas[^16]. This location allowed FTX to avoid various regulatory requirements imposed by U.S. financial authorities, complicating regulatory oversight.

Following FTX’s collapse, multiple jurisdictions engaged in legal proceedings and investigations, including U.S. federal agencies such as the Department of Justice, Commodity Futures Trading Commission, and the Southern District of New York U.S. Attorney’s Office. The Royal Bahamas Police Force also participated due to FTX’s Bahamian base[^4].

---

## Bankruptcy Process and Stakeholder Impact

FTX filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection on November 11, 2022, listing over 130 affiliated companies globally with assets and liabilities each estimated between $10 billion and $50 billion[^13]. John J. Ray III was appointed CEO to oversee the restructuring.

On May 7, 2024, FTX announced it owed approximately $11.2 billion to creditors and estimated it had between $14.5 billion and $16.3 billion available for distribution, projecting that nearly all customer funds would be repaid, with some creditors potentially receiving amounts beyond their claims[^25].

The bankruptcy process significantly affected customers, investors, and other stakeholders. Massive customer fund withdrawals led to liquidity shortages that exposed FTX’s insolvency. Investors and customers suffered substantial financial losses, and executives faced criminal charges and prison sentences[^5][^17][^22].

